#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "509fc92e-cf26-503c-bb89-8df87012e393" :question/math-academy-id "e-5767" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=6x^4-7x^3+11x-9,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-9,$ and its factors are $\\pm1,$ $\\pm3,$ and $\\pm9.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3,$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\; \\pm \\frac13, \\; \\pm \\frac12, \\; \\pm 1, \\; \\pm \\frac32, \\; \\pm 3, \\; \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, \\; \\pm 9.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm$ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2 \\cdot 8 = 16.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "12016ef4-6119-543c-adda-aa30fa7a73ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-7365" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 12x^4+5x^3-6x+15?$\n\n- $\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n- $-\\dfrac{3}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $15$ and the leading coefficient is $12.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{15}}{\\textrm{factor of }12}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\dfrac 23$ does not take the above form. The numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083020 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "20d00218-7823-5ef5-b196-a02894edc69a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7378" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 9x^4-3x^2-7x-6?$\n\n- $-\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{3}{2}$\n\n- $-9$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-6,$ and the leading coefficient is $9.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{-6}}{\\textrm{factor of }9}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(2)$ is a factor of $-6$ and the denominator $(3)$ is a factor of $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083023 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "e7c9c0d2-6af5-520a-8b56-4930780bc6df" :question/math-academy-id "e-7413" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x) =x^3 - x^2 - 4x + 4$ has a root in the interval $(0,4),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{4}}{\\textrm{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,4),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\left( x - 1\\right) (x^2 - 4).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2, 1, 2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+1+2 =1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5134e062-0027-5089-83b1-3551c78121d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313831} {:answer/id #uuid "71d8593a-7d4a-5eb0-a474-50cd1647a090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313832} {:answer/id #uuid "1537645b-ee09-5b84-aac0-1f9d900255ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313833} {:answer/id #uuid "7594d2d0-9e7c-56f8-ae61-9a9b6d92d7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313834} {:answer/id #uuid "abf1844f-d78b-5ac5-9268-9fde4db0cc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1537645b-ee09-5b84-aac0-1f9d900255ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313833} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6d24a61-9e95-58da-8486-ccec50fd2ed3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e2e3f3c-7fff-5ef4-90c6-0ff75ab0fc76" :question/math-academy-id "q-112865" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+x-6?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-6,$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-6}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{2}{3}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(2)$ is a factor of $-6$ and the denominator $(3)$ is a factor of $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b368559-cac0-5aff-8552-e7596001b3f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313837} {:answer/id #uuid "b97417f3-774e-55c7-8eed-30f60b157378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313838} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d9210d-1206-54af-b57b-2d7327999141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313839} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb84a53-da0a-5b07-a33b-3a75392d44de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313840} {:answer/id #uuid "39591534-d0ab-594b-bd85-9b13648b8d98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbb84a53-da0a-5b07-a33b-3a75392d44de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313840} :answer-field/id #uuid "366f9316-fd83-5db0-84b6-e0f42131f525" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e4c35d0-80ea-555f-a42d-fa766d0cba07" :question/math-academy-id "q-112867" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+10?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{-5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cdb747f-e75e-594a-81d3-65757c8d8e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313843} {:answer/id #uuid "478f9ce7-0c91-51ac-a291-81822f24fa8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313844} {:answer/id #uuid "917fecb8-e279-58ee-a8cf-84ec6bbd28b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\fra