/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf7a7e2-c83d-5d7f-a3be-d03c4fd46a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262777} {:answer/id #uuid "d8542cc2-cb0a-5cf8-84ad-40437a5be95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262778} {:answer/id #uuid "5100696c-1323-5af2-94ba-f71d4f2e601b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262779} {:answer/id #uuid "45e3514d-7e1e-5a50-aec2-37cd36d7b91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262780} {:answer/id #uuid "af67276e-0858-58f6-8d90-ce34dfe7c0ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8542cc2-cb0a-5cf8-84ad-40437a5be95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262778} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f68bd25-2d3e-5bc1-b52a-7f474271c701" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6029834b-7153-5587-95c0-5adfbb9eff9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112876" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=14{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-x-7?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-7$ and the leading coefficient is $14.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-7}{\\text{factor of }14}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{21}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(21)$ is not a factor of $14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dda3f2c-5004-5659-9106-0a21dd38de91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313850} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba3b5eb-68c3-5d1a-a925-e179e3828246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313851} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9e712a-f023-5a7a-bf1c-5c1c026ec7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313852} {:answer/id #uuid "e91456c2-9271-5b13-9277-3fd1f75ff31c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313853} {:answer/id #uuid "592168c9-69e7-5c37-b75f-823c75abded4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dda3f2c-5004-5659-9106-0a21dd38de91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313850} :answer-field/id #uuid "97157662-5ee7-5d66-aa2a-b74a821e6a41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb7cad97-7ee5-555b-ba50-25e926edbf58" :question/math-academy-id "q-112966" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{8}-9{x}^{4}-2x+14,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $14,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 7$ and $\\pm 14.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{14}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 14, & \\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 14.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d258c3e-e49f-50fc-9bc7-8eb4770d54d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313857} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9cbc41-20e9-57f3-a95e-a6e00a16dea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313858} {:answer/id #uuid "97c9c793-3414-53db-b6aa-cb0f04d28589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313859} {:answer/id #uuid "61ca4279-fe02-501e-8d9b-e4b2df1b454b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313860} {:answer/id #uuid "4968a692-b96b-545e-846e-99000647d563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61ca4279-fe02-501e-8d9b-e4b2df1b454b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313860} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d0b1e4-33c1-57ab-bca5-cb616e35952f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "17fefc5a-b0f8-553d-8d51-9bbf7ea2fa88" :question/math-academy-id "q-112989" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+1$ has a root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $1$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }1}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{4},1)$ is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $8$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $8$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{1}{2})(8{x}^{2}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{1}{2})(8{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{1}{2})(4{x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =(2x-1)(2x+1)(2x-1) \\\\  & =(2x+1)(2x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcbb019f-3b3a-5880-9058-8fe3b82ec1e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313864} {:answer/id #uuid "962dcb60-fc4f-5978-af78-68be7f79b1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313865} {:answer/id #uuid "310645d4-04fb-5e53-a610-278fff63446f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313866} {:answer/id #uuid "b15a90db-be51-5ab6-9ef8-0c1889e621f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313867} {:answer/id #uuid "7c1a8b22-b7c6-5a2f-9bc6-1ee8e1e5e0e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "962dcb60-fc4f-5978-af78-68be7f79b1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313865} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6460732-eee0-50fa-a379-80c2267a6f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b022a96-d200-55d8-b457-6c4636e4afcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-14144" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{8}-18{x}^{2}-33x-18,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-18,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9$ and  $\\pm 18.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $3,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 3.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{18}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{18}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm 3, \\\\ \\pm 18, & \\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3918e75-0799-5cb9-8770-42dbf6fac98d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313871} {:answer/id #uuid "1e852ee7-6741-588e-85e6-f4a92e4fdcc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313872} {:answer/id #uuid "83c24eae-d570-5578-a328-37a34871706a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313873} {:answer/id #uuid "ab210e1b-86fd-5f45-a5b1-ab61ccdcdead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313874} {:answer/id #uuid "84563e0d-92bc-5bcc-a26c-6ef3ae8c73d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3918e75-0799-5cb9-8770-42dbf6fac98d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313871} :answer-field/id #uuid "33fe66e7-d1de-5b43-b434-965bd59c248e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1eaacd47-1065-5943-8683-8557b5cb7cc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-14146" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-5x+4?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4,$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{2}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(1)$ is a factor of $4$ and the denominator $(2)$ is a factor of $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "981be7e0-9cb1-5391-aab8-6976ef5c6609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313878} {:answer/id #uuid "677c943b-bcc0-5657-a4be-8829eac0a6bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313879} {:answer/id #uuid "34603811-5418-57d0-b159-b3905ee1b53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313880} {:answer/id #uuid "fc382793-ffd0-5764-ad0d-880fbacfda05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313881} {:answer/id #uuid "1d929723-4f76-57b6-a5c3-01bab886d17e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34603811-5418-57d0-b159-b3905ee1b53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313880} :answer-field/id #uuid "f99e44d8-8647-5c5a-87e1-84b171194044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a96a00d-fbbc-5dc2-ac03-3b71bacaa76b" :question/math-academy-id "q-14148" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial  $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+10x-25?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $15$ does not take the above form. The value $15$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{15}{1},$ and the numerator $(15)$ is not a factor of $-25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124ff803-b147-51a0-97d6-091acd1bb82e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "124ff803-b147-51a0-97d6-091acd1bb82e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313885} :answer-field/id #uuid "9beaac9b-a826-51d9-b65f-a90fc0d692f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f1f33d61-0d80-57df-8e34-bef51b594085" :question/math-academy-id "q-323215" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=16{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+7x+12$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 6,$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $16,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 8,$ and $\\pm 16.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\pm \\frac{1}{16},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{16},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{8},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\  & \\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 3,\\text{ }\\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 6,\\text{ }\\pm 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $28$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 14=28.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2479bc9-6b2a-5385-99d3-9a1dbb7c48f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313888} {:answer/id #uuid "02df68ea-79bd-51b8-b773-8398857a4514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2479bc9-6b2a-5385-99d3-9a1dbb7c48f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313888} :answer-field/id #uuid "9da605cb-ae41-5ee2-8d88-220805e3f6a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95007f34-4662-5e62-8850-de706768b936" :question/math-academy-id "q-323222" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-5x-9$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-9,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 3$, and $\\pm 9.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, and $\\pm 5.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5}, & \\pm 3, & \\pm 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24dff74e-d0ac-51aa-959d-4864d68db61c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24dff74e-d0ac-51aa-959d-4864d68db61c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313892} :answer-field/id #uuid "903ad97d-d306-5752-bf8f-9d4447c8744e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7521cae-1b98-544e-8948-197abd4b00f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-323231" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-5x+10$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{10}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 5, & \\pm 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $8$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 4=8.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98b60f68-8758-5a21-9852-74557299ddcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98b60f68-8758-5a21-9852-74557299ddcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313895} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2039748-636f-5a6a-9ec7-df6cdf6723cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313894} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e02738fe-87ad-5069-8aa4-3e0eced43d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e02738fe-87ad-5069-8aa4-3e0eced43d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313897} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb121a5-e119-5d0c-933f-f2fc00e26b1a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967b2bb0-0e33-5e7b-926a-0b782b7d34b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "967b2bb0-0e33-5e7b-926a-0b782b7d34b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313899} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2198240-2089-5c73-9517-53512a0cf04c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7638975a-f83e-5fc9-ae9e-a40228be8f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-323610" :question/problem "The polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-10x-8$ has one of its roots in the interval $(3,5).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(3,5)$ is $4.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-10$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d86ad3cb-810a-5b83-8ebc-83b2d5440f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313902} {:answer/id #uuid "17db9749-e188-5b23-92f3-a51beda3f1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313903} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2446a4-ddce-59f6-a0e1-7a75a35b0f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313904} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e36e09-a73b-5c2e-8d50-46c4ff381cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313905} {:answer/id #uuid "2904b527-24b0-53da-a07b-185b187753de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2904b527-24b0-53da-a07b-185b187753de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313906} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a6ea11-34b9-55b9-b0bf-f1e42d63bdc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2adddd45-7f27-54e7-9dbc-a6a10272c38e" :question/math-academy-id "q-38251" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}+31x-15$ has one of its roots in the interval $(1,2),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-15$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-15}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 3,\\pm 5,\\pm 15,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\pm \\frac{15}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\pm \\frac{5}{4},\\pm \\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(1,2),$ are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $4$ | $-20$ | $31$ | $-15$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $6$ | $-21$ | $15$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-14$ | $10$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{3}{2})(4{x}^{2}-14x+10).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{2})(4{x}^{2}-14x+10) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-7x+5) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2x-5)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},1.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}+1=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98259c60-776f-5d69-b4e4-d293fe366d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080895} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd23e7f-a0b6-59fe-a380-b47116c9b174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080896} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad83be8-ec80-5b3f-9e53-07def80047c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080897} {:answer/id #uuid "7638e0c3-014d-55a8-90c1-4b5e439bf1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080898} {:answer/id #uuid "76dd9fc6-50df-5992-be03-4ea0c5c873fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bd23e7f-a0b6-59fe-a380-b47116c9b174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080896} :answer-field/id #uuid "1009e9ba-8a5a-5de7-b4ed-6b76962873fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f572c08e-edf8-5f9d-9827-c106bc98ece4" :question/math-academy-id "q-39033" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-15x-25$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $3$ | $5$ | $-15$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $25$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b95d111e-f7d7-5ca0-b4b8-5542af347a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313909} {:answer/id #uuid "be6c9307-6adc-5a44-b26c-474ba74b7677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313910} {:answer/id #uuid "9f620bbb-ded2-5b62-865e-cb5759e9742d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313911} {:answer/id #uuid "fa654955-243e-5212-9d47-7f0c689cb259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313912} {:answer/id #uuid "819d3f2d-23e5-5cfb-9fe3-c30be4ff81a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b95d111e-f7d7-5ca0-b4b8-5542af347a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313909} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8180927-f275-598f-a83f-bc3c11ee875c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a3e4429-09a5-5d58-a296-a07129c659bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-39036" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+35{x}^{2}+2x+14$ has a root in the interval $(-15,-6),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $14$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }14}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 7,\\pm 14,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\pm \\frac{7}{5},\\pm \\frac{14}{5}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-15,-6),$ are $-14$ and $-7.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+7)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-7$ | $5$ | $35$ | $2$ | $14$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-35$ | $0$ | $-14$ |\n|  | $5$ | $0$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+7)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+7)(5{x}^{2}+2).\n$$\n\nSince $5{x}^{2}+2\\ne 0$ for any real number $x,$ the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2df40b5-ef5d-55b4-8c23-e4c23c9b9f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313916} {:answer/id #uuid "010ac1d2-c975-5ee0-80e6-707fc69c1019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313917} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1c01ab-493a-54f1-8201-d03c1ae5e5d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313918} {:answer/id #uuid "2a088343-9fda-58ec-812b-dc117cdac915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313919} {:answer/id #uuid "8faf4508-c30f-5837-b958-369728b4315f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "010ac1d2-c975-5ee0-80e6-707fc69c1019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313917} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f604e04-2e4e-5b0e-94fe-b441485c558a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :d